@database "llm_common"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_common.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Shared code of the LLM providers"
@{h1}Shared code of the LLM providers

This plugin is the code the LLM provider plugins share: which API key may
go to which host, which HTTP statuses are worth a retry, how a cancel
reaches a running call, and the OpenAI and Gemini wire shapes more than one
route speaks. Keeping one copy is the point -- a rule like "the OpenAI key
never goes to a host the entry merely names" must be the same rule on every
route, and two copies drift.

It is a @{b}library@{ub}: no provider name, no tools, no hooks, no panel.
Nothing in @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt} names it; the provider plugins import
it. For an operator it matters in one place: an OpenAI-compatible entry
without @{tt}api_key:@{utt} gets its key by the rules in
@{" API keys and endpoints " link apikeys}.

The providers themselves are described in their own manuals:
@{" OpenAI-compatible routes " link llm_openai_compat/main},
@{" OpenAI SDK " link llm_openai/main},
@{" Anthropic " link llm_anthropic/main},
@{" Gemini " link llm_gemini/main},
@{" Ollama " link llm_ollama/main},
@{" OpenRouter SDK " link llm_openrouter/main} and
@{" Decision models " link llm_decisions/main}. Models and profiles in
general: @{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  API keys and endpoints  " link apikeys}
  @{"  What a misconfiguration looks like  " link errors}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the modules provide, and who uses them  " link modules}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node apikeys "API keys and endpoints"
@{h1}API keys and endpoints

Every OpenAI-compatible provider can be pointed at another host with
@{tt}base_url@{utt}. If a missing key simply fell back to
@{tt}OPENAI_API_KEY@{utt}, a gateway entry without a key would send the
OpenAI secret to that gateway in its Authorization header. So the key
follows the @{b}endpoint@{ub}, never the provider name.

@{h2}The order

@{bullet}The endpoint is the entry's @{tt}base_url@{utt}, or the provider's
default when it has none.
@{bullet}An @{tt}api_key@{utt} in the entry wins, whatever the host. It is
trimmed; one that is empty or only whitespace counts as not set. An unset
@{tt}${VAR}@{utt} expands to an empty string, so it counts as not set too.
@{bullet}Otherwise the host of the endpoint picks the environment variable:

@{table}
Host of the endpoint | Key taken from
openrouter.ai and its subdomains | OPENROUTER_API_KEY
api.openai.com and its subdomains | OPENAI_API_KEY
api.typesafe.ai and its subdomains | TYPESAFE_API_KEY
a local host (below) | OPENAI_API_KEY
anything else | nothing: building the client fails
@{body}

@{bullet}The variable's value is trimmed too; empty or whitespace is
"missing", and building the client fails.

The host is compared as the parsed host name, case-blind, never as text in
the URL: @{tt}https://gw.example.com/openrouter.ai/v1@{utt} is
gw.example.com, @{tt}https://api.openai.com\@evil.example/@{utt} is
evil.example, and @{tt}api.openai.com.evil.example@{utt} is not
api.openai.com.

@{h2}What counts as local

A local OpenAI-compatible server (LM Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp, Ollama's
/v1) usually accepts any key, and the secret does not leave the network, so
it gets OPENAI_API_KEY:

@{bullet}an IPv4 loopback or private address (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16,
169.254/16 and the other ranges Python calls private);
@{bullet}an IPv6 loopback (::1), unique-local (fc00::/7) or link-local
(fe80::/10) address, or an IPv4-mapped one of a local IPv4 address;
@{bullet}a name without a dot (localhost, a docker-compose or Kubernetes
service like @{tt}ollama@{utt}), @{tt}host.docker.internal@{utt}, or a
name ending in @{tt}.local@{utt}.

Not local: 100.64.0.0/10 (carrier NAT, also Tailscale), 6to4 and Teredo
IPv6 addresses (they carry a public IPv4 address), and a dotless host that
the resolver reads as an address -- @{tt}134744072@{utt} or
@{tt}0x08080808@{utt} are 8.8.8.8. Such an endpoint needs
@{tt}api_key:@{utt} in the entry.

@{h2}Which providers follow these rules

@{table}
Provider | Plugin | Default endpoint
openai | llm_openai | https://api.openai.com/v1
openai_httpx | llm_openai_compat | https://api.openai.com/v1
openai_responses | llm_openai_compat | https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
openai_speech (TTS) | llm_openai_compat | https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
openrouter_sdk | llm_openrouter | https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
openrouter_decisions | llm_decisions | https://openrouter.ai/api/alpha/decisions
systemone_decisions | llm_decisions | https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone
ollama_decisions | llm_decisions | http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/systemone
@{body}

The decision providers speak their own wire, not OpenAI's: a
@{b}local@{ub} endpoint without @{tt}api_key@{utt} gets no key at all
instead of OPENAI_API_KEY, and the request goes out without
Authorization.

Not covered here: @{tt}anthropic@{utt} reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, the Gemini
providers GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY, Ollama needs none (see their
manuals), and the OpenAI batch backend always talks to api.openai.com (see
@{" API keys and endpoints " link llm_openai_compat/apikeys}).

@{h2}Example

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    deepseek-direct:
      provider: openai_httpx
      model: deepseek-chat
      base_url: https://api.deepseek.com/v1
      api_key: ${DEEPSEEK_API_KEY}   # required: api.deepseek.com is in no table
    local-qwen:
      provider: openai_httpx
      model: qwen3
      base_url: http://vllm:8000/v1  # dotless: local, OPENAI_API_KEY if set
@{body}
@endnode

@node errors "What a misconfiguration looks like"
@{h1}What a misconfiguration looks like

Both failures are a @{tt}ValueError@{utt} when the client is built -- the
first turn with that model fails, before anything is sent. What the chat
and the fallback chain do with it is the provider's and the core's
business; the text is this plugin's.

@{h2}A host with no variable

The entry names a host the table does not know and sets no
@{tt}api_key@{utt}:

@{code}
provider=openai_httpx targets https://api.deepseek.com/v1, and no
environment variable is configured for that host — set `api_key:` on the
model entry (e.g. api_key: ${YOUR_PROVIDER_API_KEY}). Falling back to
OPENAI_API_KEY would send that secret there.
@{body}

Fix: @{tt}api_key: ${DEEPSEEK_API_KEY}@{utt} on the entry. If that variable
is unset, the key expands to an empty string and you get this message
again.

@{h2}The host's variable is missing

@{code}
OPENROUTER_API_KEY is required when provider=openai_responses targets
https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
@{body}

The variable is unset, empty or only whitespace. Set it, or set
@{tt}api_key:@{utt} on the entry.

@{h2}What the messages never contain

No key and no part of one: they name the variable, never its value. A
password in the URL's user part (@{tt}https://user:secret\@host/v1@{utt}) is
cut from the URL in the message; the host and path stay.
@endnode

@node modules "What the modules provide, and who uses them"
@{h1}What the modules provide, and who uses them

Something belongs here when two or more provider plugins would otherwise
copy it. Code only one provider needs stays in that provider.

@{table}
Module | Provides | Used by
api_keys | @{tt}resolve_api_key(api_key, base_url, *, default_base_url, provider, local_fallback=True)@{utt} returns @{tt}(key, effective_url)@{utt}; @{tt}key_var_for(url)@{utt} names the variable for a URL or None | llm_openai, llm_openai_compat (chat, Responses, speech), llm_openrouter, llm_decisions
http_status | @{tt}RETRYABLE_STATUS@{utt}: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, Cloudflare's 520-524, 529 | llm_openai_compat (speech), llm_decisions
cancellation | @{tt}await_call(task, token)@{utt}: awaits a provider call while watching the cancel token; a cancel arrives as @{tt}asyncio.CancelledError@{utt}, and the call is stopped and awaited when the caller is cancelled | llm_openai, llm_anthropic, llm_ollama, llm_decisions
model_dialects | the per-model dialect keys (@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt}, @{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt}, @{tt}thinking_request_shape@{utt}, @{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt}, @{tt}prompt_cache_marker_style@{utt}): allowed values, validation at construction, @{tt}warn_unwired@{utt} for a key a route ignores, @{tt}reasoning_replay_flags@{utt} | all six provider plugins
openai_utils | message and content normalisation for the OpenAI wire: images, text files, audio to @{tt}input_audio@{utt}, API fields only | llm_openai, llm_openai_compat
structured_output | the Chat Completions @{tt}response_format@{utt} and the Responses @{tt}text.format@{utt} of a structured-output request | llm_openai, llm_openai_compat
schema_sanitize | @{tt}sanitize_schema_for_gemini@{utt}: strips JSON Schema keywords Gemini's function declarations refuse, keeps limits as text in the description | llm_gemini, and llm_openai_compat through the @{tt}gemini_function_declarations@{utt} dialect
@{body}

@{h2}Using resolve_api_key in a new provider

@{code}
from plugins.llm_common.api_keys import resolve_api_key

api_key, base_url = resolve_api_key(
    cfg.api_key, cfg.base_url,
    default_base_url="https://api.example.com/v1",
    provider="example")
@{body}

Pass @{tt}local_fallback=False@{utt} when the wire is not OpenAI's: a local
endpoint then gets @{tt}""@{utt}, and the client must send no
Authorization header for an empty key. A new host with its own variable is
one line in @{tt}_HOST_KEYS@{utt} in @{tt}api_keys.py@{utt}.

An unknown dialect value raises at construction:
@{tt}tool_schema_dialect='x' is not a declared value for model 'm' —
expected one of [...]@{utt}. A new value is added in
@{tt}model_dialects.py@{utt} only; the config literal must follow (a test
compares them).
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable. @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} declares
@{tt}type = ["library"]@{utt} and no @{tt}provides@{utt}, so the LLM
registry skips it; the manifest exists so the dependency aggregation and
the import-declaration guard cover the package. No
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} entry, no allowlist line, no dependencies
beyond the core.

The environment variables it reads are the three in
@{" API keys and endpoints " link apikeys}; put them in the environment or
the secrets file the server loads. Nothing on disk.

@{h2}Tests

@{tt}src/plugins/llm_common/tests/@{utt}; @{tt}test_api_keys.py@{utt} uses
fake keys only and sends nothing.
@endnode
